    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d282">

                <pb facs="0415" n="345" sameAs="frus:frus1862/pg345"/>

                <head>Mr. Dayton to Mr.
                        Seward.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 154.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-06-02"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >June</hi> 2, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatches Nos. 151, 152, 153, 154,
                    155, 156, 158, and 159 have been received.</p>
                <p>No. 151, while acknowledging that France has faithfully practiced the neutrality
                    she has proclaimed, again refers to the anxious desire felt for a withdrawal
                    from the insurgents of the concession of belligerent rights, and despatch No.
                    154 indirectly refers to the same thing. I have already informed you to what
                    extent this point has been pressed upon the attention of the French government,
                    and scarcely suppose you desire me, under existing circumstances, to go further.
                    Indeed, after what has been said here, I do not see how it is possible to do so
                    at present. I shall await some encouraging intimation from Mr. Adams of a change
                    of purpose in England, or something from Mr. Mercier, through you, before I
                    shall venture upon the question again, unless, in the meantime, I am otherwise
                    directed. I repeat that it is in vain to hope that France, so long as the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">entente cordiale</hi> between the two countries
                    continues to exist, will in this matter separate her policy from that of
                    England.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WM. L. DAYTON.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>His Excellency <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward,</hi><hi
                            rendition="#italic"> Secretary of State, &amp;c., &amp;c.,
                        &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
